/*! \page mi_neuray_example_entity_resolver Example for a custom MDL entity resolver

<div align="right">
    [\link mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering Previous\endlink]
    [\link mi_neuray_examples Up\endlink]
</div>

This example shows how to implement a custom MDL entity resolver.

\section mi_neuray_example_entity_resolver_new New Topics

  - Virtual file system abstraction
  - Resolving modules
  - Resolving resources
  - Limitations of the example

\section mi_neuray_example_entity_resolver_descr Detailed Description

<dl>
<dt><b>Virtual file system abstraction</b></dt>
<dd><br>
The example uses an \c IFilesystem interface as an abstraction of a file system to demonstrate which
operations are necessary to implement a custom entity resolver. At the minimum, one needs to be
able to check for the existence of files, and to open files. Two implementation are provided:
\c Os_file_system directly passes all queries to \c fopen(), and \c Virtual_file_system serves all
queries from its own state. The first one is used by the default, the second one can be enabled by
passing \c --virtual_fs to the example.
<!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
</dd>

<dt><b>Resolving modules</b></dt>
<dd><br>
A successful resolver operation for modules returns an instance of
#mi::neuraylib::IMdl_resolved_module. Such an implementation returns the name of the module, its
filename, and provides a reader. The reader should be constructed lazily.

The resolver operation starts with a check for \c "." and \c ".." as module name components. In
practice, the compiler enforces these rules for modules. It is still repeatedly here for clarity
and consistency with resources, which \em do need these checks.

Absolute module names can be simply looked up in the search paths. A relative module name is
converted into an absolute module name and filename using the provided owner name and owner file
path. However, a simple existence check is not sufficient here. If the module exists, we still need
to check whether it is shadowed by an absolute module name (see "Check 1" in section 2.2. of [\ref
MDLLS]). Note that for correct support of weak-relative module names, a single error message \em
needs to be provided in the context.
<!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
</dd>

<dt><b>Resolving resources</b></dt>
<dd><br>
For simplicity, this example does not implement support for uv-tiles or animated textures. A
successful resolver operation returns an instance of #mi::neuraylib::IMdl_resolved_resource which
references (given these limitations) a single instance of
#mi::neuraylib::IMdl_resolved_resource_element. It also means that when implementing the former
interface, the file path mask is identical to the file path, and the filename mask is identical to
the filename. For resources, it is strongly recommended to construct the reader lazily when
requested (or implement the reader in such a way that all I/O operations like opening the
underlying stream done lazily).

The resolver operation starts with a check for \c "." and \c ".." as file path components. For
resources, these checks are actually mandatory since they are not enforced by the compiler.

The remainder of the resolver operation for resources is quite similar to that of modules. Absolute
resource file paths can be simply looked up in the search paths. A relative resource file path is
converted into an absolute resource file path and filename using the provided owner name and owner
file path. However, a simple existence check is not sufficient here. If the resource exists, we
still need to check whether it is shadowed by an absolute resource file path (see "Check 1" in
section 2.2. of [\ref MDLLS]).

Note that resolving of resources requires an additional error message compared to modules (where
the compiler adds one in a generic way).
<!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
</dd>

<dt><b>Limitations of the example</b></dt>
<dd><br>
As mentioned, the example does not support uv-tiles or animated textures. Adding support for that
requires extending the file system abstraction to support the enumeration of directories. In
settings where that is not easily possible, the resolver needs to fall back to probing potential
files for existence, which can be expensive and is error-prone.

Furthermore, the implementation of #mi::neuraylib::IMdl_resolved_resource_element needs to be
extended to support an array of uv-tiles, including the mapping from to index to u- and
v-coordinates. The implementation of #mi::neuraylib::IMdl_resolved_resource needs to be extended to
support an array of animated textures. The correct file path mask and filename mask need to be
returned.

A second limitation of this example is the lack of support for MDLE and MDL archives. Adding support
for that requires a larger extension of the file system abstraction to deal with containers and
members thereof. In particular the implementation of these functions can be quite involving. Both
require additional consistency checks. In addition, the lookup rules for MDLE are completely
different.
<!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
</dd>
</dl>

